    "q-106910": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106910",
      "name": "Question 17",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. $C[0,1],$ the vector space of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 300}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 300$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}_{20}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $20$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $C[0,1]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}\\},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in [0,1]$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $C[0,1]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $C[0,1]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 300}(R)$ and ${R}_{20}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{200\\times 300}(R))=200\\cdot 300=60\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{20}[t])=20+1=21\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
