    "q-106916": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106916",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${ℓ}^{1},$ the vector space of infinite real sequences where the sum of the absolute values of the terms are finite\n\nII. ${R}_{3000}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3\\,000$\n\nIII. ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R),$ the vector space of all $50\\times 60$ matrices over $R$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${ℓ}^{1}$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ is linearly independent: \n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${ℓ}^{1}$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${ℓ}^{1}$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${R}_{3000}[t]$ and  ${\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R)$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{3000}[t])=3\\,000+1=3\\,001,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{50\\times 60}(R))=50\\cdot 60=3\\,000\n$$\n\n \nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
