    "q-106908": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106908",
      "name": "Question 19",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${R}^{900}$\n\nII. ${R}_{100}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $100$\n\nIII. ${R}^{\\infty },$ the vector space of infinite real sequences",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${R}^{900}$ and  and ${R}_{100}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed,  are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{900})=900,\n$$\n\nand\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{100}[t])=100+1=101,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\n- ${R}^{\\infty }$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of sequences is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\{{e}_{1},\\,{e}_{2},\\,…,{e}_{n}\\},\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nwhere, for each $j\\in \\{1,2,…,n\\},$\n\n$$\n{e}_{j}=(0,…,0,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{1,}_{{↑}_{{j}^{\\text{th}}\\text{ position}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0,…).\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{e}_{i}=0\n$$\n\nif and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of ${R}^{\\infty }$ of size $n,$ which imples that  ${R}^{\\infty }$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
