    "q-95385": {
      "dom_id": "step-q95385",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R),$ the vector space of all $400\\times 400$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. $C[-1,1],$ the vector space of all continuous functions $f:[-1,1]\\to R$\n\nIII. ${R}^{400}$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $C[-1,1]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}\\},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in [-1,1]$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $C[-1,1]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $C[-1,1]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{400}(R)$ and ${R}^{400}$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{400}(R))={400}^{2}=160\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{400})=400\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-95385-a",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I and II only",
              "html": "I and II only"
            },
            {
              "option": "b",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-95385-b",
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II and III only",
